    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "**Limitations in Error Mitigation Protocols: The Role of Crosstalk in NISQ Devices**\n\nThe study's methodology is compromised by an inadequate consideration of crosstalk during gate operations. Crosstalk refers to the unwanted exchange of information between adjacent qubits or gates, which can lead to errors in quantum computations (Veldhoven et al., 2018). Specifically, ZZ (Pairs Annealing)\\(^2\\) interaction, a type of crosstalk, can significantly impact the fidelity of NISQ devices.\n\nZZ interaction occurs when two adjacent qubits are used in a two-qubit operation (e.g., CNOT or Hadamard gate). This interaction can result in a loss of phase coherence, leading to errors in load and prepare operations (Johnson et al., 2017). The effects of ZZ interaction on NISQ devices can be particularly detrimental, as it can not only add noise to the system but also affect the comptuling and post-processing paths. In their work, the authors' neglect to account for this type of crosstalk likely inflated their success rates in preserving quantum coherence.\n\n**Critical Assessment of Statistical Significance: Modeling the Impact of Crosstalk**\n\nTo assess the statistical significance of the authors' results",
      "source": "target",
